  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nThe sun is shining and a look out the window (and most area \nwebcams) show the sky with a brilliant blue color. Temperatures\nhave climbed into the mid 60s under the sunshine with upper 60s\nexpected for the high temperature today. GOES satellite water \nvapor imagery shows an amplifying wave over the eastern Dakotas \nand west-central Minnesota presently. Showers and isolated \nstorms accompany the reflected surface front with the wave and \nare expected to track east through the overnight. The forecast \nleaned on the HRRR/RAP solutions (as they are doing a pretty \ngood job at current conditions in the northern Plains) which \nkeeps most, if not all, of the precipitation in Wisconsin \ntonight. A sliver of slight chance PoPs were kept in the \nnorthern tier counties just south of the Wisconsin state line, \nbut given the amount of dry air over Illinois, confidence is\nhigher in just a few sprinkles compared to a wetting rain.  \nSoundings show lapse rates aloft that are not terrible \novernight, but instability should weaken as the system crosses \nthe Mississippi River keeping the risk for lightning at bay. The\nsystem should mostly pass over harmlessly, switching southeast \nwinds to the northwest behind the front and ushering in a \ncooler, drier air mass.\n\nAny mid-level cloud cover that moves over northern Illinois \ntonight with the system should help quell any localized shallow \nfog chances tomorrow morning. As winds turn to the northwest, \ntemperatures will fall into the low 40s. Given the northerly \nflow expected tomorrow, high temperatures were nudged down \nslightly, but with cloud cover decreasing through the day, \ntemperatures should once again return to the upper 60s with \nareas along and south of I-88 reaching the low 70s. Clearer \nskies are expected tomorrow night which will help drop \ntemperatures back down to the low 40s with rural areas having a \nbetter chance at touching the upper 30s.\n\nSeasonable temperatures will prevail through the week. High pressure \nwill help maintain mostly drier conditions, although another \nwave over the northern Plains heading to Wisconsin mid-week \nwill be monitored. Guidance continues to favor the track of the \nsystem north of Illinois so, for now, no precipitation is in the\nforeseeable future. \n\nDK",
